    <h1 class="fade-up">Garden design in <em>Oxfordshire</em> for family country homes</h1>
    <p class="lead fade-up d2">Garden design in Oxfordshire for family homes in the country: generous borders, orchards, meadows and kitchen gardens that sit naturally in the landscape.</p>
    <div class="hero__actions fade-up d3"><a class="btn" href="/contact.html">Discuss your garden <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div>
  </div></div>
</section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap prose-grid">
  <article class="prose reveal"><p class="lead">A country garden in Oxfordshire has something no town garden has: the landscape around it. Good <strong>garden design in Oxfordshire</strong> borrows the views, the stone and the hedgerows, and makes a garden that feels as if it has always been there.</p>
<h2>Reading the site</h2>
<p>From the limestone of the Cotswold edge to the clay of the Thames valley, Oxfordshire soils vary within a few miles. We start every country project with the soil, the aspect, the wind and the views, because these decide which plants will thrive with little help and which will struggle.</p>
<h2>A garden that steps down into the landscape</h2>
<p>Close to the house, a country garden can be quite formal: terraces in local stone, clipped yew, a kitchen garden or a rose garden. Further out it should loosen, with long grass, orchard trees and native hedges, until it blends into the fields beyond. This gradient is what makes the best English country gardens feel settled.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Terrace and borders</strong> near the house, planned for long seasons of colour.</li>
<li><strong>Kitchen garden</strong> with raised beds, a cutting garden and a greenhouse.</li>
<li><strong>Orchard and meadow</strong> with mown paths through long grass full of bulbs and wildflowers.</li>
<li><strong>Native hedging and trees</strong> for shelter, wildlife and a link to the countryside.</li>
</ul>
<figure><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/meadow-path.webp" alt="Mown path running through long meadow grasses and wildflowers" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Mown paths through long grass bring the meadow right up to the garden.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Planting for a family in the country</h2>
<p>Families in the country want space to run, and planting that does not need daily attention. Long-lived perennials, shrub roses, grasses and spring bulbs give a lot of colour for the effort. Crab apples such as <em>Malus x atrosanguinea</em> 'Gorgeous' flower in spring and hold their fruit into December, so they earn their place twice.</p>
<div class="pull">Close to the house, order. Further out, let the landscape take over.</div>
<div class="duo"><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/dahlias.webp" srcset="/assets/img/dahlias-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/dahlias.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Coral, peach and cream dahlias in full bloom" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/allium-field.webp" srcset="/assets/img/allium-field-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/allium-field.webp 1400w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Purple alliums standing above green foliage" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><h2>Across Oxfordshire and beyond</h2>
<p>We work across Oxfordshire, the Cotswolds, Berkshire and Buckinghamshire, as well as in Suffolk and Sussex, and visit regularly during the build and the first growing seasons.</p></article>
  <aside class="aside">
    <div class="aside-card reveal"><h3>At a glance</h3><dl><div><dt>Typical size</dt><dd>0.25 to 3 acres</dd></div><div><dt>Best for</dt><dd>Family homes in the country</dd></div><div><dt>Season of interest</dt><dd>All year</dd></div><div><dt>Signature plants</dt><dd><div class="palette"><span>Malus &#x27;Gorgeous&#x27;</span><span>Yew hedging</span><span>Rosa &#x27;Kew Gardens&#x27;</span><span>Persicaria</span><span>Sanguisorba</span><span>Wildflower meadow</span></div></dd></div></dl></div>
    <div class="aside-card aside-card--dark reveal"><h3>Planning a country family garden?</h3><p>Tell us about your space and we will arrange a consultation visit.</p><a class="btn" href="/contact.html">Get in touch <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:.9rem">Or email <a href="mailto:info@fraserandmorris.com" style="color:#fff">info@fraserandmorris.com</a></p></div>
  </aside>
</div></section>
<section class="section section--cream"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head reveal" style="justify-content:center;text-align:center"><div><p class="eyebrow">Questions</p><h2>Country family garden FAQs</h2></div></div>
  <div class="faq"><details class="reveal"><summary>Do you work outside London?</summary><div class="answer"><p>Yes. As well as London we design gardens across Oxfordshire, the Cotswolds and the Home Counties, and in Suffolk and Sussex.</p></div></details><details class="reveal"><summary>How long does a country garden take to establish?</summary><div class="answer"><p>Hedges and perennials look established after two or three growing seasons; trees and meadows take longer. We plan the planting so the garden looks good in its first summer while the structure matures.</p></div></details><details class="reveal"><summary>Can you help with a kitchen garden?</summary><div class="answer"><p>Yes. We design productive gardens with raised beds, fruit, cutting flowers and greenhouses, laid out so they are attractive as well as practical.</p></div></details></div>
</div></section>
